racle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-gate-clarity.md", ".prawduct/changes/gate-clarity.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "The Stop gate's third arm has no test for the guards that keep it from failing open: untracked files count as authored, and an edited imported file stays required", "recommendation": "`_imported_history_verdict` does its own `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard` read and passes the result into `_session_authored_paths`. That function's final `git diff HEAD` never lists untracked files, so this list is the only way an uncommitted new file the session wrote counts as authored. The change record and the plan's narrowed [DECISION] both claim 'every untracked file counts as authored' and 'a file both authored and imported stays required'. Neither claim has a session-level test: `TestHistoryThisSessionDidNotWrite` builds no untracked file, edits no imported file, and never makes the `ls-files` read fail. The algebra-level `test_a_gap_holding_a_refused_file_is_not_excused` covers the predicate only, not this wiring. Consequence: if the untracked list were dropped or misread, a session with a new unreviewed `x.py` plus a base merge would get a gap of {x.py, b.py}. The gate would excuse that gap and pass, with a NOTE calling x.py 'a sync brought in'. No test would go red. Fix (instance): add three cases to `_imported_history_session`: (1) an untracked judgeable file must stay uncovered and be named among 'files this session wrote'; (2) a session edit to the merged-in `b.py` must stay uncovered; (3) an `ls-files` failure must leave the verdict unchanged. Red-verify (1) by passing `[]` for `untracked`.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "tests/test_session_critic_gate.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "rule-unenforced: anchored insert landed the imported-files NOTE between Gate 2.5's header comment and Gate 2.5's code", "recommendation": "Scope: instance (the class is mechanizable). Why it broke: the new `imported` NOTE block in cmd_stop was anchored on the line after the Gate 2.5 header comment ('# Gate 2.5: Advisory mode gate ... Advisory NOT blocking ... Reads the session work span'). So that header now sits over the imported NOTE, and the mode-gate `if critic_has_work and not critic_doc_only and not critic_would_block:` it describes has no header. A maintainer who edits, moves or retires Gate 2.5 by its header takes the wrong block with it. Fix the instance by moving the imported-NOTE block, with its own comment, above the '# Gate 2.5' comment so that header again sits directly over its code. This is the core.md rule 'A text-anchored edit changes a NEIGHBORHOOD, not a point', which no check enforces. To mechanize it, a diff-aware record/lint check would flag any hunk that inserts code between an unchanged comment block and the code line that block directly preceded at the base. The diff hunk positions are enough to compute that.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "changes_out_of_remit is defined and rendered as 'non-Python' in the verifier-generic gate, not as 'outside this verifier's remit'", "recommendation": "Scope: class, the field's definition across its three homes. Why it broke: the bucket was named for the Python symbol-grep floor's case, but `verify_coverage` and the field's documentation serve every verifier, and project-state-keys.md tells products to 'plug in a stronger verifier'. Three places tie the field to the floor's language. The gates.py `_EVIDENCE_OPTIONAL_FIELDS` comment says it 'lists changed non-Python files'. project-state-keys.md defines it as 'non-Python files'. The `verify_coverage` fallback reason, used when a record carries no `changes_out_of_remit_reason`, says 'not Python: the symbol-grep floor reads only Python'. A language-native verifier, such as a Swift one, has no verifier-neutral bucket for the prose and config it never reads. It either keeps putting them in `changes_unjudged`, which is the #682 confusion again for every non-Python product, or it emits `changes_out_of_remit` without a reason and the gate tells the operator its files are 'not Python'. Define the field relationally ('changed files outside the producing verifier's remit; the record's `changes_out_of_remit_reason` says why'), with the floor's non-Python rule as one instance. Make the gate's fallback reason neutral ('outside the verifier's remit'), so the floor's own reason text lives only in `test-reference-verify`'s OUT_OF_REMIT_REASON.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "Two gate-semantics decisions merge still marked 'Inferred, not ruled'", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: the plan's Goals section records two [DECISION]s as 'Inferred, not ruled' and hands them to the boundary review rather than to the owner. These are (a) the #1098 relax-only third arm in gates.session_review_verdict / _imported_history_verdict, which lets the Stop gate pass a session span that is otherwise uncovered, and (b) the Chunk 04 narrowing of `changes_unjudged`, which the plan's own api-contract disposition calls an 'exception' to the additive-first norm. Both are recorded and vetoable, so this is not a Goal 3 departure. But one relaxes a BLOCKING gate and the other takes an exception to a ratified norm, and the owner has not seen either. If this merges as it stands, the next reader of the plan cannot tell whether the gate relaxation is owner policy or one builder's inference. Fix: put both decisions to the owner before merge, record the answer in `.prawduct/owner-rulings.md`, and update each marker to say it was ruled. A veto of (a) means removing the third arm. A veto of (b) means restoring non-Python paths to `changes_unjudged`.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/bin/test-reference-verify"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "The Stop gate's imported-history arm declines without saying why", "recommendation": "Class, bounded. Why it broke: `_session_authored_paths` returns a bare `None` for all of its failure modes. These are an unrecognised reflog subject (`_classify_reflog_subject` returns None for any wording outside its list), a reflog that does not reach `.session-start`, a missing session-start stamp, and any failed git call. `_imported_history_verdict` also returns the original verdict without comment in three other cases: `ls-files` fails, the excused-gap search diffs no pair, or `EXCUSED_GAP_MAX_PAIRS` is reached. In every one of these cases the builder who synced and is still blocked sees the same uncovered message as before #1098, with nothing to show that the excuse was tried and why it did not apply. Of the arm's outcomes, only 'authored files are unreviewed' adds to the reason. A consumer whose git logs a reflog subject the classifier does not know will meet the #1098 friction again, and neither they nor a maintainer reading the output can find the cause without reading the code. Fix: have the authorship read return its failure reason instead of a bare None. Then append that reason, along with 'search cap reached' or 'no interval could be diffed', to the verdict's `reason` when the arm declines. The two callers to check are `_session_authored_paths` in `session_work_span` and `_imported_history_verdict`.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py"], "fid": "R-5"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 5, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "The uncommitted-path read uses rename detection, so the stash route can still fall short on a staged rename", "recommendation": "`coverage._uncommitted_paths` runs porcelain `git diff --name-only -z HEAD`, which honours `diff.renames` (on by default). After a staged `git mv a.py b.py` of a suite-coupled file it lists only `b.py`. The PR gate's new remedy then says `git stash push -- b.py`, which leaves the staged deletion of `a.py` in place. The suite run still fails to meet HEAD and the gate denies again, which is the 'remedy that cannot reach the state' #1000 set out to remove. Rare, and the gate stays fail-closed. Fix (instance): add `--no-renames` to that call. The `tree_diffs` docstring already handles the same config hazard for plumbing.", "files": ["plugin/lib/coverage.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "Chunk 01's acceptance asked for a warm-Stop timing; the record measures the diagnosis alone", "recommendation": "The plan's acceptance for Chunk 01 is 'a warm-Stop timing before and after, on this repo and on one consumer clone'. The decisions and the change record report 'diagnosis only, warm' timings instead. The record says so openly, so nothing was silently dropped, and the diagnosis is the cost #937 named. Noted only so the boundary reader knows the whole-Stop figure was never taken.", "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Governing-Artifact Reconciliation: every recorded disposition is true of the diff", "recommendation": "I checked every disposition in the plan's `governed_by` against the code. 01 re-asks per pair whatever git skips, malformed ids included, and persists nothing. 02 never raises and degrades to `CAUSE: unavailable (...)`. It changes no verdict or exit code, and its additions are stderr or blocker text. 03 relaxes only `uncovered` at the marker span, returns the verdict untouched when `_session_authored_paths` or `ls-files` fails, and caps its pair search so the cap can only deny. The PR gate's verdict is untouched. 04 keeps `changes_unchecked` as an exact alias of `changes_out_of_remit`. The one departure, the narrowing of `changes_unjudged` against additive-first, is recorded as a vetoable [DECISION] and disposed of as an exception, not as conforming. No departure lacks a recorded decision. Note that both [DECISION]s (#682 narrowing, #1098 third arm) say 'Inferred, not ruled': the owner has not yet seen either choice, which the plan itself flags for the boundary.", "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Probes: test evidence is current and coverage enforcement is off", "recommendation": "`prawduct-hook test-status` exited 0: a run on feature/gate-clarity at 2026-11-12T01:36:46Z met this exact tree. `verify-coverage` exited 0 with 'skipped: coverage_required is false'. Test deletions in the diff are deliberate contract changes, each replaced by a stronger or parametrised case: the old 'unchecked is a subset of unjudged' invariant is inverted by #682 and re-pinned as the four-bucket partition plus the alias-equality tests. No assertion was weakened without a reason.", "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Untracked files are enumerated again where an enumeration is already in hand", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `_imported_history_verdict` runs its own `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard -z`, but `session_review_verdict` already holds `capture['untracked']` from `evidence.capture_tree`. That is the same command, against the same index, at the moment the target tree was captured. The extra call also runs before the cheap `_session_authored_paths` early return. Passing `capture['untracked']` through (and keeping today's verdict when it is None) removes a working-tree walk and keeps the arm's untracked set consistent with the tree it judges. `coverage._uncommitted_paths` is another copy of the same enumeration and is optional to fold in.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/coverage.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The 'first three paths, then (+N more)' elision is now copied in three new places, one of them beside the helper this change created", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Search for `more)\" if len(` across plugin/lib and plugin/bin; earlier copies already sit in coverage.py. This change adds `coverage._shown` with `_CAUSE_PATHS_SHOWN`, then inlines the same idiom with a literal 3 in `gates._imported_history_verdict` and in prawduct-hook's imported NOTE. Route the new sites through `coverage._shown`, or a public equivalent, so 'up to three paths' has one owner. Nothing breaks today.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/coverage.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "explain_uncovered(target=) and format_uncovered_cause(gate=) carry one distinction in two unvalidated vocabularies", "recommendation": "`target='working'|'head'` and `gate='stop'|'pr'` must always be passed as a matched pair. Any other value silently falls back to the PR behaviour in one function and the PR wording in the other. Both callers in this change pair them correctly. A single gate identifier on the explanation dict, which the formatter reads, would make a mismatched pair unrepresentable.", "files": ["plugin/lib/coverage.py"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "_reach copies _find_path's graph construction instead of sharing it", "recommendation": "`_reach`'s docstring claims 'the same node set and the same free-edge relation `_find_path` searches', and the claim holds because the code was copied. The two already differ: `_reach` never runs `_attribute_free_steps`, so free steps on the third arm's covered path have no `files`. No consumer reads that path today, so nothing is wrong now. Extracting a shared `_evidence_graph(edges, endpoints, key_fn)` would keep the two in step. Soundness is not at risk, because edge validity comes from the shared `review_edges` and `_free_classes`.", "files": ["plugin/lib/coverage_algebra.py"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "read-cost: the Stop gate's CAUSE line re-reads the whole evidence store that session_review_verdict just read", "recommendation": "`session_uncovered_cause` calls `evidence.read_facts` again on every uncovered Stop block, and the store only grows. The `read_facts` docstring itself warns against a second open, because a sibling's append between the two reads decouples them. The cost is paid only while the gate blocks, and no volume is stated, so this is an observation. Passing the facts the verdict already read through cmd_stop would remove the second read without making the session-start briefing pay for the explanation (see `templates/data-model.md` 'Access Paths').", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Shipped comments narrate history and pin inert timings", "recommendation": "coverage.py ('one line of a single batched git call since #937', '#937: ~140 diffs, ~2.9 s of a warm Stop') and the `evidence.tree_diffs` docstring ('#937: ~140 diffs, ~2.9 s ...') carry history and a measurement nothing reads. The plan and change record are those figures' home. Delete them when the code is next touched. No dedicated edit is wanted.", "files": ["plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "review-cycle.md's one-sentence description of the Stop gate omits the new imported-history arm", "recommendation": "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md line 18 says the Stop gate asks whether coverage spans the session base tree to the working tree. That remains true as the primary question, and the existing fallbacks were never listed there either. No reader acts wrongly, because the gate prints its own NOTE when it excuses imported files. No edit is required.", "files": ["plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md"], "oid": "O-11"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Removing the deprecated `changes_unchecked` alias has no tracking home", "recommendation": "The alias is said to be kept 'until the next major release, then removed'. That intent is written only in test-reference-verify's docstring and comment and in the change record. The api-contract's Deprecation & Compatibility section does not list evidence-record keys, so a major-release cut has nothing that enumerates what is due for removal. Nothing gates on this. The alias is harmless if it stays.", "files": ["plugin/bin/test-reference-verify"], "oid": "O-12"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "New shipped comments narrate issue history and inert timings", "recommendation": "Three new shipped comments carry history. The `tree_diffs` docstring says '(#937: ~140 diffs, ~2.9 s of a warm Stop, growing with the review facts)'. The condition-2 comment in `diagnose_base_advance_transfer` says 'since #937' and repeats '~140 diffs, ~2.9 s'. The `changes_unchecked` comment says 'the name this list had before #682'. The figures are inert, nothing reads them, and they will go stale. Prose ceiling: an observation, and no edit is needed this round. If these comments are touched later, cut the figures and the 'since #N' wording.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/bin/test-reference-verify"], "oid": "O-13"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass: covered all three oracle files; none clears a bar", "recommendation": "Records Pass over files_oracle: .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-gate-clarity.md, .prawduct/changes/gate-clarity.md and .prawduct/owner-rulings.md. I checked the change record's mechanism claims against the code: the batched diff and its per-pair fallbacks (a skipped pair, a malformed id, a failed or unparsable batch, a path set with a newline), the CAUSE line's four causes and its `unavailable` render, the stash route, the third arm's conditions (relaxes only `uncovered`, untracked counts as authored, authored+imported stays required, a blocker blocks), and the four-way partition with its alias. Each claim matches the code, and each new rendered message has an assertion on its text. Below any bar: the plan's 'Level: Medium for 03' was not raised after the Chunk 03 read it names, and the PR-sized timing reads '0.15 s' where the plan says '0.15\u20130.18 s'. Also below any bar: the new owner-rulings entry says the owner 'set aside the seeded-defect test repo until 3tears-evals is ready', which the quoted owner sentence does not contain. That entry supersedes the previous day's ruling that #1147's requirements are next. If the deferral came from another message, quote those words so a later session does not have to take the narration on trust.", "files": [".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-gate-clarity.md", ".prawduct/changes/gate-clarity.md"], "oid": "O-14"}], "duration_seconds": 900, "scope": "gate-clarity", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01", "02", "03", "04", "05"], "chunks_proposed": ["01", "02", "03", "04", "05"], "chunk": "01", "base_reviewed": "9e93e8bdf2b49b74c9123fcad6de1e06bbf188d1", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-gate-clarity.md", ".prawduct/changes/gate-clarity.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md"], "chunk_graded": "01", "chunks_graded": ["01", "02", "03", "04", "05"], "plan_graded": ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-gate-clarity.md", "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": 0, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body": 0, "learnings-area-dead": 0, "change-fragment": 0, "retired-claim-survives": 0}}, "dispatched_at": "2026-11-12T01:42:20Z"}}

